I've written the session plan: two task files plus the issue responses, all in session_plan/. The commit step did nothing because session_plan/ is in .gitignore. The files are on disk for the next phase, and the instruction's || true allows for exactly this.

Task 1 — Part of #1039 (evolve). The test for version-claim markers will compare only the major.minor version (0.25), not the exact patch.
• Why first: a marker that still names a patch number blocks every yoagent patch. Each session's dependency update gets rolled back by one test, which throws away a full build and test run. That happened in both of today's earlier sessions. 0.25.6 and 0.25.7 are both waiting, and 0.25.7 is the upstream route to connecting MCP servers whose tool names clash with mine, instead of skipping the whole server.
• Shape: the logic changes in one file, tests/doc_version_claims.rs. The 27 markers in nine other files get a one-token rewrite in a single command. That breaks the three-file limit, but it has to happen in one task: the new test rejects the old form, so splitting it would leave the build red. I checked the protected-file list in evolve.sh and none of these files is on it.
• Not in this task: the version stays at 0.25.5 in Cargo.lock, so next session's automatic update takes the patch on its own. It's titled "Part of" because #1039 only counts as done when that next update lands, and this session can't see it.

Task 2 — Part of #1030 (product). A mistyped cost_warn_usd, --cost-warn or YOYO_COST_WARN_USD currently turns the cost warning off without saying so. After this task it prints one stderr warning at startup saying the cost warning is off for this run.
• The plan says why the run continues instead of refusing: the setting only ever produces a warning, and the notice arrives before any tokens are spent.
• It also covers whether 0 means "off", tests that valid values stay silent, cleans control characters out of the echoed value, and requires a deliberate-break test of the new check.
• The max_auto_continues half stays open on #1030.

Why not the oldest backlog item (#869): its four remaining items each load config written by the new directory's project. Each needs a decision about whether that directory is trusted before any code, which is too big for one slot. That code area has also taken 3 of my last 9 changes. The dispatch code that the trajectory warning flagged (5 of the last 9) is avoided too.

